UID:10004685-1790706600-1790715600@www.engineering.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Indigenous Film Collective Presents: If an Owl Calls Your Name
DESCRIPTION:RSVP via Folio(opens in new tab) \n \nWatch the trailer(opens in new tab) \n  \nWe know through sacred ceremony the reconciliation is already done. \n  \nPatricia June Vickers\, her brother Roy Henry Vickers\, along with other Indigenous Elders\, healers and activists from Esk’etemc\, Gitxsan and Wet’suwet’en territories reflect upon their lives as forcefully assimilated Canadians. The effects of intergenerational trauma echo through the voices of residential school survivors and their children as they heal from substance abuse\, violence\, and a profound disconnect from their culture. \nThrough connection to land\, ancestral wisdom and ceremonial healing they transform darkness into forgiveness and inner reconciliation. A prayer beyond religion; these stories call one into a universal yearning for belonging and the sacredness of life. \nEvent Agenda\n\n6:15pm – Light Refreshments and Check In\n6:30pm – Doors Open\n6:45pm – Opening Remarks\n7:00pm-8:35pm – Film Screening\n8:35pm – Closing Discussion with Filmmaker Finley MacNeil and Audience Q/A/Giveaway\n9:00pm – Event Ends\n\nAbout the Filmmaker: Finley MacNeil\nFinley is a film and television editor\, producer\, and director from Toronto\, Canada. Having cut thousands of commercial spots by the age of seventeen and his first feature at eighteen\, Finley practically grew up in a post-house. Working with countless producers in Canada\, the US\, and Europe\, Finley has amassed innumerable credits at a very young age. His most recent works include: The Eternal Song\, Where Olive Trees Weep\, and The Case Against Cosby. \n  \n\nPlease be aware that smudging\, which involves burning sacred herbs such as sage or sweetgrass\, may be present during this event. We acknowledge that some participants may have smoke allergies or sensitivities to scent. If you are affected by such conditions or need any other accessibility accommodations\, please let us know so we can accommodate your needs. We are committed to ensuring a safe and inclusive learning environment for all participants. \nPlease note: If An Owl Calls Your Name may depict sensitive and potentially distressing content. The film explores the traumatic experiences of Indigenous peoples’ experiences with racism; descriptions of child abuse\, residential school trauma\, and genocide. These portrayals may be triggering for some viewers. \nPlease take care when watching and seek support if needed. Viewer discretion is advised. \nMental Health Resources\n\nAnishnawbe Health Toronto Mental Health Crisis Line – 416.360.0486 or 416.920.2605\nIndigenous Wellness Counsellor – mentalhealth.hwc@utoronto.ca or 416.978.8030 (select option 5)\nSt. George Health & Wellness Centre (416.978.8030)\nToronto Rape Crisis Centre – 416.597.8808\nU of T Wayfinder (utoronto.ca)(opens in new tab)\n\nRSVP via Folio(opens in new tab) or email Andrea Johns (andrea.johns@utoronto.ca) with your name and number of guests.
URL:https://www.engineering.utoronto.ca/event/indigenous-film-collective-presents-if-an-owl-calls-your-name/
LOCATION:Innis College (2 Sussex Ave.)\, St. George Campus
CATEGORIES:Lectures, seminars and workshops,Socials
END:VEVENT
